:: warrior_queen_day_2_1

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   DAY 2 — STANCE AND CENTER
   Part 1: Morning — The Body's Report
═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

I woke to pain.

Not sharp pain — not the bright, clean sting of a cut or the hot flare of a bruise. This was //structural// pain. The deep, grinding complaint of muscles that had been asked to do things they had never done, hold positions they had never held, and support a body that was not the body they had been trained for.

My core ached. The band of muscle wrapping my midsection, from the bottom of the ribs to the top of the hips, felt as though it had been beaten with rods. My lower back throbbed with each breath. My thighs — //inner// thighs, specifically, the muscles that had been engaged by yesterday's wide-legged power stance and then punished for their inadequacy — were so stiff I could barely separate my legs.

And the breasts were //sore//.

I lay on my back in the half-light of dawn and felt them aching. Not the tenderness of growth, which had become a constant companion these past weeks — a low, humming sensitivity that lived in the tissue like a second heartbeat. This was something layered atop the growth-ache: the soreness of flesh that had been compressed by an ill-fitting chest piece, bounced during two hours of failed swordsmanship, and then squeezed further by Maera's binding. The tissue was swollen. I could feel it without touching — a heaviness, a //fullness//, as though the breasts had responded to yesterday's abuse by doubling their efforts to make themselves known.

I placed my hand over my left breast, gingerly, through the sleeping shift. The contact was immediate: warm, soft, //yielding//. The mound was — was it larger than yesterday? I pressed gently. The tissue compressed beneath my fingers, dense and tender, and the nipple — hidden beneath shift and binding but undeniably present — sent a pulse of sensation inward that made me snatch my hand away.

<<speech "mc">>Do not,<</speech>> I told myself. <<speech "mc">>Do not catalogue. Do not measure. Do not compare to yesterday. Get up. Dress. Move.<</speech>>

I sat up — and the breasts shifted.

The binding had loosened in sleep, as bindings do, and the tissue that had been compressed against my ribs all night now //dropped// slightly as gravity reasserted itself. A small motion. A quarter-inch, perhaps. But I felt it — felt the weight transfer, felt the flesh settle into a new resting position, felt the gentle, pendulous tug that came with having mass on the chest that responded to gravity independently of the ribcage beneath.

It was the most female sensation I had experienced since the transformation began. Not the tenderness. Not the growth. The //settling//. The way the breast tissue had its own relationship with gravity, separate from the body it sat upon, obeying its own physics.

I sat on the edge of the bed with my feet on the cold stone floor and let the sensation settle with it.

[[Get dressed for training|warrior_queen_day_2_2]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_2

Maera had left a package outside my door. I found it when I opened the chamber to fetch wash-water from the corridor cistern — a wrapped bundle of linen, tied with a neat bow, with a note in her precise hand:

//Proper binding. Wider. Stiffer. Sewn ties. Will not slip. Put it on BEFORE the leathers. And eat something — there is bread and cheese in the cloth.//

Beneath the binding was indeed bread and cheese, wrapped in a separate cloth. Maera. Always practical. Always one step ahead.

I unwound the old binding — stiff with dried sweat, loose and useless — and examined the new one. It was clearly her own work: three layers of linen, stitched together for stiffness, with reinforced edges and fabric ties at both ends that could be knotted securely. The width was generous — it would cover from below the armpits to below the breast curve. And she had, I noticed with a flush of gratitude, sewn a double layer of padding into the section that would lie over the nipples.

She had thought about my nipples. She had //planned// for them. Had sat somewhere in her chamber last night, needle in hand, and thought: //Caelan's nipples will chafe against the leather if I do not pad this section.//

The mortification and the gratitude arrived simultaneously, tangled together like fighting cats.

I stripped the sleeping shift over my head — flinching again as the fabric dragged across the nipples, because they were //always// flinching now, every contact a small announcement of their existence — and stood naked in the cold morning air.

The breasts tightened immediately. The cool air found them like a lover's breath, and the nipples hardened — puckering, contracting, the areolae crinkling into taut little peaks that stood out from the small mounds of flesh like sentinels on alert. I could //see// the goosebumps forming on the breast skin, spreading outward from the nipple in concentric rings, the tissue responding to cold in a way that the flat male chest had never responded to anything.

I wrapped the new binding quickly. The stiffer fabric held better — it compressed the breasts firmly against the ribcage, the padded section settling over the nipples and cushioning them. The ties cinched at my back and held. I tested it — bouncing on my heels, twisting at the waist, swinging my arms in a rough approximation of a sword stroke. The binding held. The breasts were contained. The nipples, padded, did not scrape against anything.

<<speech "mc">>Thank you, Maera,<</speech>> I murmured. Then, quieter: <<speech "mc">>Thank you for knowing what I needed before I knew it myself.<</speech>>

I pulled on the training leathers. The chest piece sat better today — not well, not correctly, but better. The binding's compression reduced the small mounds to a flatter profile, and the padding prevented the nipple impressions that had horrified me yesterday. I could almost pass for the prince I had been.

Almost.

The trousers were the same negotiation as yesterday — the shimmy over widening hips, the tug over a backside that was stubbornly, persistently //rounder//, the lacing at the waist that needed to be tighter as the waist narrowed and looser as the hips flared. But the binding held, and the bread was fresh, and I ate it standing in my chamber with cheese crumbs on my chin and something that was not quite courage but was at least its second cousin settling into my chest alongside the aching breasts.

[[Head to the Iron Garden — Day 2 training|warrior_queen_day_2_3]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_3

The Iron Garden at dawn. Mist again — Velmoria bred mist the way other kingdoms bred horses, abundantly and without apology.

Aldric was already present. Of course he was. The man did not arrive — he //was there//, as though the yard generated him along with the mist each morning.

But today he carried no sword. In his hands were two things: a wooden dowel, the length of a practice blade but round and smooth, and a sack of sand roughly the size of a large melon.

I eyed the sack with a suspicion that proved, within moments, to be richly justified.

<<speech "mc">>What purpose does that serve?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Your chest.<</speech>>

No inflection. No amusement. No malice. Three syllables, delivered with the emotional warmth of a trebuchet stone.

<<speech "aldric">>Strap it on.<</speech>>

He tossed the sack to me. I caught it — reflex, the hands still fast even if they were softer — and held it at arm's length, staring at it as though it were a live serpent.

The sack was weighted to approximately four pounds. It was shaped, roughly, like a pair of breasts — two lobed masses of sand connected by a leather bridge. It had straps. //Chest straps//. Designed to be worn against the torso.

Aldric was asking me to strap a pair of false breasts to my chest.

Over the real, growing, aching ones.

[[Strap on the sand sack|warrior_queen_day_2_4]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_4

The heat that flooded my face could have forged steel.

<<speech "mc">>You are jesting.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I do not jest. Jesting requires a sense of humour, which I am reliably informed I do not possess. Strap it on.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, I already have — I mean, there is already —<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>What you //already have// is a pair of buds that weigh, by my estimation, two or three ounces each. What you //will have//, within months, is a pair of breasts that weigh a pound or more apiece. I am not training you for the body you have today. I am training you for the body you will wear to the throne. Strap. It. On.<</speech>>

I stared at him. He stared back. It was like staring at a cliff face and expecting it to blink.

I strapped it on.

The leather ties went around my torso and over my shoulders, securing the sand sack against my chest. The weight settled immediately — four pounds of sand, divided into two heavy lobes that pressed directly onto the binding, which pressed onto the budding breasts beneath, which pressed onto the hypersensitive tissue that was growing, swelling, and extremely displeased about being subjected to additional compression.

I bit back a hiss. The added pressure on flesh that was already swollen and tender sent bright threads of pain stitching through my ribs, down through the nipples — even through Maera's careful padding — and deep into the pectoral muscle beneath. The sand shifted with each breath, the lobes swinging slightly, pulling at the straps, generating a constant, rhythmic tug against the chest that was nothing like the body's own weight and everything like carrying two soft, heavy burdens that the body had not asked for.

<<speech "mc">>This is...<</speech>>

I did not finish the sentence. //Humiliating// was the word. //Degrading//. //Correct//. All three were true.

<<speech "aldric">>Heavy?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>...Aye.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Good. This is what your chest will carry in six months. Perhaps more. Every stroke, every parry, every step and lunge and pivot will carry this weight. The sooner your body learns to account for it, the sooner the weight becomes invisible. Now — <</speech>>

He set his feet and sank into a stance I had never seen him demonstrate.

<<speech "aldric">>Today, you shall learn the Velmorian Feminine Guard.<</speech>>

[[Learn the Feminine Guard|warrior_queen_day_2_5]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_5

<<speech "mc">>The what?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The Velmorian Feminine Guard. An ancient fighting stance designed for the female body. Used by the Queen's personal guard three centuries past, before the tradition was lost to time and the arrogance of men who decided that if women wished to fight, they could bloody well fight like men.<</speech>>

That was the longest continuous speech I had heard Aldric deliver in approximately three years. I blinked.

<<speech "aldric">>The male power stance — the one drilled into every soldier since the day he picks up a practice sword — does the following. Feet wide. Weight in the thighs. Shoulders braced. Center of gravity high, in the chest and upper body. Force generated through mass — the heavier the man, the harder the stroke. This stance was designed for a body with broad shoulders, narrow hips, a flat chest, and a high center of mass. It is, quite simply, a stance built for men.<</speech>>

He paused.

<<speech "aldric">>You are not becoming a man.<</speech>>

The silence that followed was so absolute I could hear the mist condensing on the cobblestones.

<<speech "aldric">>The Feminine Guard does the opposite. Feet closer together. Weight in the hips — //the hips//, not the thighs. Spine straight, elbows tucked. Center of gravity low, in the pelvis and the core. Force generated not through mass but through rotation — the wider the hips, the stronger the pivot. The lower the centre, the stabler the base. The lighter the frame, the faster the strike.<</speech>>

He demonstrated. And despite the incongruity — a man built like a castle tower adopting a stance designed for a woman's body — the demonstration was flawless. His body sank low. His weight pooled visibly in his core. His feet drew closer together, precisely positioned, and his elbows tucked to his sides. He became, in the space of three seconds, a different kind of fighter — not smaller, but more //concentrated//. More //dense//. As though every ounce of him had been compressed into the narrow column between hip and shoulder.

<<speech "aldric">>This stance was designed for bodies with lower centres of gravity. Wider hips. Narrower shoulders. Weight distributed through the midsection rather than the upper body. It was designed —<</speech>>

He looked at me.

<<speech "aldric">>— for the body you are growing. Now. Assume the stance.<</speech>>

[[Attempt the stance|warrior_queen_day_2_6]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_6

I set my feet closer together. Attempted to drop my weight into the hips — into //the hips//, as though I knew where the hips were, as though the body's new architecture came with a map and a set of clearly labelled instructions.

The hips were wider. I //knew// this. I felt it every time I sat in a chair that was now too narrow, every time trousers that had once slid on easily now required negotiation. But //finding// them as a source of strength, as the foundation of a fighting stance, was an entirely different proposition.

I sank. Too far. My knees buckled.

<<speech "aldric">>Not the knees. The hips. Sink //into// them. Imagine your pelvis is a bowl. Pour your weight into the bowl.<</speech>>

I tried again. Sank. Wobbled. The sand sack swung forward with the motion, its four pounds of false breast weight dragging my balance toward my toes. I lurched, caught myself, and felt my actual breasts compress against the binding as the sand sack pressed inward.

<<speech "mc">>The sack is —<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The sack is showing you what your body will do in six months when you descend into guard. The chest weight will shift your balance forward. Compensate. Lean //back// into the hips. Let the pelvis take the weight that the chest creates.<</speech>>

I leaned back. The hips widened — or rather, I let them //be// wide, stopped fighting the new angle of the femur, stopped trying to force the legs into the narrow track of the male stance. The wider pelvis accepted the weight that the sand sack was pulling forward. A counterbalance. Physics.

<<speech "aldric">>Spine straight. Elbows in. Core engaged — tighten the band of muscle around your waist. Feel it? That is your armour. That is what holds the centre when the limbs are in motion.<</speech>>

I engaged the core. The muscles there — muscles I had never consciously used in combat — fired reluctantly, sluggishly, like servants roused from a long sleep. But they fired. The midsection tightened. The wobble reduced.

<<speech "aldric">>Now breathe. Into the belly. Not the chest. The belly.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I have always breathed into the chest.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Aye. Because the male stance roots strength in the upper body, and chest-breathing expands the ribcage and braces the shoulders. The feminine guard roots strength in the centre. Belly-breathing engages the core, stabilises the pelvis, and —<</speech>> he gestured at the sand sack <<speech "aldric">>— prevents the chest weight from oscillating with each breath. If you breathe into the chest, the breasts will move. If the breasts move, the balance shifts. If the balance shifts during a parry, you are dead. Breathe. Into. The belly.<</speech>>

I breathed into the belly.

The difference was immediate. The chest stilled — the sand sack's swinging reduced, the false breasts held in place by a ribcage that was no longer expanding and contracting with each breath. The core engaged deeper. The belly filled with air, and the weight pressed //downward// rather than outward — into the hips, into the pelvis, into the widening cradle of bone that was, whether I liked it or not, becoming my body's true centre of power.

[[Hold the stance|warrior_queen_day_2_7]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_7

I held the stance. For ten seconds. Twenty. Thirty.

At twenty minutes, my lower back was screaming. Sweat ran in rivers down my spine, soaking the linen beneath the sand sack. Every bead of moisture found the binding over my breasts and turned it into a warm, damp compress against tissue that was tender enough to register the temperature change. My thighs trembled — not the massive quads of the old power stance, but the inner thighs, the //adductors//, muscles I had barely been aware of possessing, now burning with the effort of holding the narrower foot position.

My core... my core was //on fire//. The band of muscle Aldric had told me to engage was alight with a deep, structural ache — not the surface burn of a hard workout, but the foundational agony of infrastructure being rebuilt. These muscles had slept for twenty years. They belonged to the body I was becoming, and waking them was like waking a beast that had been hibernating since birth.

But I held the stance.

Shaking. Drenched. Jaw clenched hard enough to crack teeth. The sand sack pulling forward, my hips pulling back, the core holding the balance between them like a keystone in an arch.

I. Held. The. Stance.

Aldric circled me. Slowly. With the patient, predatory orbit of a man assessing a weapon's readiness. He pressed my left shoulder — testing the stability. The shoulder gave slightly and I compensated from the core, the correction automatic, and I saw something flicker in his grey eyes.

He tapped my right hip with the dowel.

<<speech "aldric">>Half an inch forward. The hip must be under the shoulder, not behind it.<</speech>>

I adjusted. The half-inch shift changed the weight distribution and suddenly the stance felt //different// — not easier, but more //aligned//. As though the body had been waiting for this specific configuration and was sighing with a relief I could feel in the bones.

He pressed his palm flat against my lower back. The contact was impersonal — the hand of an armourer adjusting a plate — but the touch still jolted through me. His hand was warm. Large. Calloused. Pressed against a spine that was thinner now, the vertebrae more prominent, the muscle on either side leaner, and the //curve// of the lower back more pronounced — the lumbar lordosis that Thalara had told me would deepen as the pelvis tilted to accommodate its new width.

<<speech "aldric">>Straighten here. The arch is natural — your spine is changing to match the hips — but in the guard you must control it. Too much arch and your back breaks before your opponent does.<</speech>>

I straightened the lower back. The adjustment pulled the pelvis forward, which pulled the hips under, which anchored the weight more firmly into the stance, and —

Something //clicked//.

[[The moment it clicks|warrior_queen_day_2_8]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_8

Not in the body. In the //understanding//.

For one crystalline, perfect moment, the stance //aligned//. Weight sinking through hips into earth. Core engaged and alive, a pillar of controlled tension running from ribs to pelvis. The sand sack's weight distributed evenly, no longer a burden pulling me forward but a component of the system, accounted for and balanced by the counterweight of the hips behind. My breath low, slow, anchored in the belly, the chest still, the heart beating steady behind breasts that were — for one extraordinary second — not an obstacle but simply //part of the machine//.

I felt //rooted//. Not rigid like the old stance, which had planted me like a post to be battered against. Rooted like a willow — flexible, centered, alive with the potential of motion. Every direction was available. Every angle was possible. The stance did not anchor me to a spot — it anchored me to //myself//, to the centre, to the place where the body's power truly lived.

In the old stance, power had come from the shoulders and thighs — the //outside// of the body, the big muscles, the loud voices. In this stance, power came from the //centre// — the core, the hips, the deep architecture of pelvis and spine. It was quieter. More internal. And it used the very things I had been fighting against — the wider hips, the lower centre, the shifted weight — as //assets// rather than obstacles.

The wider hips were not a problem. They were a //base//.
The lower centre was not a weakness. It was //stability//.
The weight on the chest was not a burden. It was //information// — a signal the body could learn to read and compensate for, the way a sailor reads the wind.

For one moment, I understood. Not intellectually — the understanding went deeper, into the muscles, into the bones, into the slowly reshaping architecture of a body that was, for the first time, being asked to fight //with// itself rather than against.

Then my left calf cramped.

I collapsed sideways with a yelp that escaped at a pitch I did not recognise as my own — higher, breathier, almost //feminine// — and the shock of hearing that sound from my throat was nearly as disorienting as the cramp itself.

The yelp echoed off the Iron Garden's stone walls. A crow on the rampart cawed in what sounded like commentary.

I lay on the cobblestones with one leg seized in a knot of cramping muscle and one hand pressed against the sand sack — which had shifted during the fall and was now resting against my actual breasts with the full four pounds of sand compressing the tender tissue directly through the binding, and the pain was //exquisite// — and I stared at the grey sky and thought: //That yelp was a woman's sound. That sound came from a woman's throat. And it came from me.//.

[[Aldric's response|warrior_queen_day_2_9]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_9

Aldric stood over me. Arms folded. Naturally.

He did not extend a hand. He never did. Offering help would have been an acknowledgment that the fall was significant. In Aldric's world, falls were not significant. Only the rising.

I dragged myself upright. The calf was still cramping — a vicious knot beneath the skin — and I bit my lip hard enough to taste copper. The sand sack had resettled against my chest at an angle, one lobe pressing directly onto the left nipple through the binding. Even through Maera's careful padding, the concentrated pressure on that singular point of sensitivity sent a bright, sharp signal through the breast that was halfway between pain and something I refused to name.

I adjusted the sack. Shifted it back to centre. The nipple throbbed in protest, then settled.

I assumed the feminine guard. Without being told. Without instruction.

Feet together. Weight in hips. Core engaged. Spine straight. Breath in the belly.

The stance held. The calf protested. I overruled the calf.

Aldric circled. He watched. He said nothing for a time that felt longer than the morning.

Then something moved behind those grey eyes — something that, in a more expressive man, might have been approval. In Aldric, it was barely a shift, a softening of the granite by a fraction of a degree. But I had been studying this man's face for a decade, and I knew his expressions the way a sailor knew weather signs. That was approval.

He gave a single nod.

The nod.

In my entire life of training beneath this man — years of drills, sparring, bruises, and the occasional concussion — I had never received more than that nod. It was his highest currency, spent with a miser's precision. Worth more than any medal, any praise, any ribbon the court could bestow.

I locked it away inside my chest like a gemstone. Right beside the aching breasts, in the space that was neither prince nor queen but something being forged between the two.

<<speech "aldric">>Again. Hold for sixty seconds.<</speech>>

I held for sixty seconds. Shaking. Dripping. Teeth clenched. The sand sack a weight I was learning to carry. The breasts beneath it a weight I was learning to acknowledge.

Sixty seconds. Not a wobble.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

[[Break for water|warrior_queen_day_2_10]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_10

The break came at midmorning. Aldric permitted water and bread — he was not, despite all appearances, actually trying to kill me — and I consumed both while standing, because sitting would have constituted admitting that my legs could not support me.

My legs could not support me. But the principle was important.

I leaned against the weapons rack and drank from the clay jug, water running down my chin and dripping onto the chest piece. The leather was dark with sweat. The binding beneath was thoroughly soaked. The breasts — my actual breasts, small and sore and compressed — ached with a steady, warm throb that pulsed in counterpoint to my heartbeat.

Footsteps from the yard entrance. I straightened — an instinct, the prince's instinct, //do not be caught leaning// — and immediately regretted it as my core muscles howled.

Nyssa appeared.

She came around the corner barefoot, as always, her layered robes covered in star patterns that seemed to move in the peripheral vision. The crystal orb floated at her shoulder, humming a soft C-sharp that meant, if I remembered correctly, //curious//.

<<speech "nyssa">>Oh! There you are. Thalara sent me — she wishes to know how the body is responding to the accelerated regimen. Something about monitoring the pectoral tissue development under physical stress conditions.<</speech>> She tilted her head, birdlike. <<speech "nyssa">>Why do you have a sack of sand strapped to your chest?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Training.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>It looks as though someone has given you enormous false bosoms.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>That is //precisely// what someone has done, Nyssa. Thank you for the observation.<</speech>>

She stepped closer, peering at the sand sack with the scientific fascination she brought to everything — no embarrassment, no judgment, just relentless, cheerful curiosity.

<<speech "nyssa">>How heavy?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Four pounds.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Hmm. That is approximately the weight of a D-cup bosom on a medium frame, accounting for tissue density and mammary gland volume. Thalara's projections for your final size are —<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Nyssa. I do not wish to know my projected final breast size whilst standing in a training yard with sand strapped to my chest.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Fair. But Caelan — may I?<</speech>>

She gestured toward the sand sack. I stared at her.

<<speech "mc">>May you //what//.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Feel the weight distribution. Thalara wishes to know if Aldric's simulation is anatomically accurate. The sand should sit higher on the chest for early-stage development — your tissue is currently budding in the upper pectoral region, and the final weight distribution will not descend to a natural hang until the fourth or fifth month of growth.<</speech>>

She said this with the same cheerful matter-of-factness that she might use to discuss the properties of a herb or the migratory patterns of geese. Nyssa did not experience embarrassment. The concept existed in her vocabulary the way "unicorn" existed in the vocabulary of someone who had never seen one — technically understood, practically irrelevant.

[[Let Nyssa examine the sand sack|warrior_queen_day_2_11]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_11

I sighed. The sigh was higher-pitched than it would have been a month ago.

<<speech "mc">>Fine. But be quick about it.<</speech>>

Nyssa's hands found the sand sack — small, quick, precise. She poked the left lobe, squeezed it, lifted it, let it drop. The motion jostled the straps, which jostled the binding beneath, which jostled the actual breast beneath //that//, and I sucked in a breath through my teeth.

<<speech "nyssa">>The lobes are too low,<</speech>> she announced. <<speech "nyssa">>The weight should sit here —<</speech>> she pushed the left sand-lobe upward, pressing it higher on my chest, approximately where the budding breast tissue actually sat beneath the binding <<speech "nyssa">>— not here. At your current stage of development, the tissue is still high and firm, sitting atop the pectoral muscle. The natural droop comes later. Sir Aldric has simulated the weight of a //mature// bosom but positioned it for a //settled// one. The simulation should match your trajectory, not the destination.<</speech>>

She adjusted the right lobe as well, pushing it higher. The repositioning changed the weight distribution — the pull on my balance shifted from a forward drag to a higher, tighter compression against the upper chest. It was still heavy. Still unfamiliar. But it sat where the actual breasts sat, and the body's response was subtly different — less like carrying an external burden and more like an amplification of what was already there.

<<speech "nyssa">>There,<</speech>> she said, stepping back. <<speech "nyssa">>That is more accurate. You should feel the weight higher now — closer to the collarbone, pulling the shoulders forward rather than the chest down.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I feel it.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Good. The shoulders will want to round forward to compensate. Resist the urge — rounded shoulders compress the developing tissue and can affect growth shape. Stand //tall// with the weight. Let the chest carry it proudly rather than collapsing around it.<</speech>>

She paused, then added, in a tone that was softer, almost conspiratorial:

<<speech "nyssa">>Caelan. For what it is worth — the tissue development is beautifully on track. Symmetrical, clean margins, excellent vascularity. Thalara is very pleased. Your body is responding to the alchemy with remarkable... enthusiasm.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Enthusiasm,<</speech>> I echoed. <<speech "mc">>My body is //enthusiastically// growing breasts.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Vigorously, even! The growth rate in the past week has exceeded projections by approximately fifteen percent. If it continus at this pace —<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Nyssa.<</speech>>

<<speech "nyssa">>Right. Not in the training yard. I shall relay to Thalara that the simulation is adjusted and the subject is surviving Sir Aldric's tender ministrations. Carry on!<</speech>>

She whirled and departed in a flutter of star-patterned robes, the crystal orb trailing behind her humming a cheerful E-flat.

I stood in the Iron Garden with an adjusted sand sack and the knowledge that my breasts were growing fifteen percent faster than expected, and thought: //Enthusiasm. The word for it is enthusiasm.//.

Aldric reappeared from wherever he had retreated during Nyssa's visit — likely the shadows, where he maintained a permanent reservation.

<<speech "aldric">>The mage's apprentice has adjusted the sack.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>She said the weight was positioned too low.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Hmph.<</speech>> A pause. <<speech "aldric">>The correction is sound. She is irritating but not incompetent. Resume the stance.<</speech>>

[[Resume training — afternoon session|warrior_queen_day_2_12]]


:: warrior_queen_day_2_12

The afternoon was an education in endurance.

Aldric did not teach new techniques. He did not introduce weapons. He simply made me hold the feminine guard — again, and again, and again. Five minutes. Rest. Five minutes. Rest. Seven minutes. Rest. Ten minutes. Rest. The rest periods grew shorter as the holds grew longer, and my body, already exhausted from the morning, descended into a kind of trembling, sweating, teeth-gritting purgatory that was not quite pain and not quite effort but some unholy marriage of both.

The sand sack was a constant companion. I was beginning to develop a relationship with it — the kind of relationship a prisoner develops with a ball and chain. It swung when I shifted. It pulled when I leaned. It compressed the binding and the breasts beneath during each hold, generating a low-grade ache in the chest tissue that became, over the hours, a background drone I was learning to filter out.

Not ignore. //Filter//. There was a difference. Ignoring the breast ache meant pretending it was not there. Filtering meant acknowledging it, cataloguing it, and filing it under "sensations the body produces that are not emergencies and do not require immediate response." It was, in its way, the first step toward //integrating// the breasts into the body's operating system rather than treating them as foreign invaders.

At some point during the afternoon — I had lost track of time, lost track of everything except the stance and the burn and the breath — Aldric began to speak.

<<speech "aldric">>The body you are building is not weaker than the body you are leaving. It is //different//. The old body was a fortress — thick walls, heavy gates, built to withstand siege. The new body is a river — it does not resist force, it //redirects// it. A fortress stands still and endures. A river flows around every obstacle and wears down mountains given time.<</speech>>

He circled me. I held the stance. The sweat ran.

<<speech "aldric">>The masculine fighting tradition treats the body as a weapon of mass. Mass is strength. Mass is power. Mass is dominance. The man with the most mass wins. This is not wrong — for men. For a body with broad shoulders and narrow hips and a flat chest and a high centre of gravity, mass IS the advantage.<</speech>>

