Feet together. Weight in hips. Core engaged. Spine straight. Breath in the belly. Sand sack settled high on the chest, pulling the shoulders forward — resist the pull, stand tall, let the weight be carried rather than collapsed into.

Ten seconds. The stance was solid. The body was remembering yesterday's moment of //click// — the alignment, the flow, the willow-rootedness.

Twenty seconds. The tremor began in the left calf — the same one that had cramped earlier. I adjusted. Shifted a fraction of weight to the right. The left calf settled.

Thirty seconds. The core was burning. The muscles I had never used were screaming their displeasure at being asked to work a second consecutive day after twenty years of dormancy. I breathed into the belly. The core held.

Forty seconds. The sand sack began to feel different. Not lighter, but... //integrated//. Part of the system. The weight no longer pulled me forward — it sat atop the stance like a load on a well-built cart, balanced, distributed, //accounted for//. My body had found the compensation pattern. The hips countered the chest. The core held the bridge.

